## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question blends sliding-window technique with binary search to analyze a threshold problem, testing practical algorithm design and reasoning about complexity.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window for fixed-length subarray sums
- Binary search over the answer space
- Time/space trade-offs and edge-case handling
- Practical optimizations (early exit, optional prefix sums)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLWithSumAtMost(A, T) {
  const n = A.length;
  const ok = (L) => {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < L; i++) sum += A[i];
    if (sum <= T) return true;
    for (let i = L; i < n; i++) {
      sum += A[i] - A[i - L];
      if (sum <= T) return true;
    }
    return false;
  };
  let lo = 1, hi = n, ans = 0;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (ok(mid)) { ans = mid; lo = mid + 1; } else { hi = mid - 1; }
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would the solution change if elements could be negative?
- What are the memory/speed trade-offs if you precompute prefix sums?
- How would you adapt for multiple thresholds T1..Tk in parallel?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests running-statistics in streaming data and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- Running median with two heaps; balance invariant; tie handling for even n.
- Complexity: O(log n) per insert, O(1) median; memory O(n).

## Code Example
```python
import heapq
class MedianMaint:
    def __init__(self):
        self.L = []  # max-heap via negatives
        self.R = []  # min-heap
    def add(self, x):
        if not self.L or x <= -self.L[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.L, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.R, x)
        if len(self.L) > len(self.R) + 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.R, -heapq.heappop(self.L))
        elif len(self.R) > len(self.L):
            heapq.heappush(self.L, -heapq.heappop(self.R))
    def median(self):
        if len(self.L) > len(self.R):
            return -self.L[0]
        return (-self.L[0] + self.R[0]) / 2
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a sliding window median?
- What are memory trade-offs if n grows large?
- How would you extend to compute other statistics simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming cardinality under tight memory; evaluates practical choices and error budgeting.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog, register updates, relative error, memory sizing.
- Trade-offs: more registers → better accuracy but higher memory; batch size impacts latency minimally.

## Code Example
```javascript
function hllAdd(hash, regs, m){
  const idx = (hash >>> (64 - Math.log2(m))) & (m - 1);
  const w = countLeadingZeros(hash) + 1;
  regs[idx] = Math.max(regs[idx] || 0, w);
}
```

